1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,475
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,475 --> 00:00:08,774
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:08,774 --> 00:00:14,594
3M, ticker MMM never reached the trigger, so no position was taken.

4
00:00:14,594 --> 00:00:18,416
The entry did its quiet job: it kept us out.

5
00:00:18,416 --> 00:00:23,352
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

6
00:00:23,352 --> 00:00:28,761
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

7
00:00:28,726 --> 00:00:32,255
On the day, the book is up 903 dollars.

8
00:00:32,255 --> 00:00:36,327
Central Bancompany (CBC) leads, +3.3 percent.

9
00:00:36,327 --> 00:00:40,218
Diamondrock Hospitality (DRH) lags at -0.4.

10
00:00:40,218 --> 00:00:45,105
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

11
00:00:45,105 --> 00:00:48,631
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

12
00:00:48,587 --> 00:00:57,051
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

13
00:00:57,051 --> 00:01:05,965
12 positions, led by AbbVie, ticker ABBV, Diamondrock Hospitality, ticker DRH, Chevron, ticker CVX.

14
00:01:05,965 --> 00:01:18,210
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

15
00:01:18,169 --> 00:01:22,812
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

16
00:01:22,812 --> 00:01:25,134
Every morning OROS proposes.

17
00:01:25,134 --> 00:01:35,333
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

18
00:01:35,333 --> 00:01:39,148
Inside the book today: 9 positions up, 3 down.

19
00:01:39,148 --> 00:01:41,469
Best, ACIW at +4.33 percent.

20
00:01:41,469 --> 00:01:43,128
Worst, DRH at -0.39.

21
00:01:43,128 --> 00:01:47,937
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

22
00:01:47,937 --> 00:01:54,902
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -3.16% per trade over 1195 graded positions.

23
00:01:54,902 --> 00:02:01,702
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

24
00:02:01,702 --> 00:02:06,677
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

25
00:02:06,677 --> 00:02:21,934
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 166 names close the day in Stage 2; 2 new invalidations queue exit reviews; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

26
00:02:21,884 --> 00:02:24,149
Over to the sports desk.

27
00:02:24,149 --> 00:02:33,491
Day 79 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

28
00:02:33,491 --> 00:02:39,813
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 105,091.

29
00:02:39,813 --> 00:02:45,380
Then the index fund, in gray, 3.2 thousand back at 101,940.

30
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:52,646
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 7.5 thousand back at 97,607.

31
00:02:52,646 --> 00:02:55,288
The race has no finish line.

32
00:02:55,288 --> 00:02:59,629
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

33
00:02:59,629 --> 00:03:04,536
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

34
00:03:04,536 --> 00:03:06,423
Nothing is restated.

35
00:03:06,423 --> 00:03:15,331
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

36
00:03:15,293 --> 00:03:22,628
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

37
00:03:22,628 --> 00:03:24,870
Nothing here is financial advice.

38
00:03:24,870 --> 00:03:27,858
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
